expand: a backslash inside a format directive is an invalid character

Format read a backslash escape even in the middle of a directive, so the
escape was printed and the directive went on with whatever followed it.
Digits on both sides of the escape then made up a single width:

	Format(nil, `%1\A00x`, []string{"0"})  // "\A" and then "0" padded to 100

bash and dash reject such a format:

	$ printf '%1\A00x' 0
	bash: printf: `\': invalid format character
	$ dash -c "printf '%1\A00x' 0"
	dash: 1: printf: %1\: invalid directive

Escapes are now only read outside of directives, and a backslash inside
one fails with "invalid format char: \". FuzzFormat found it once its
bound followed the format; the input is kept as a seed.

Reproducer, before this change: Format(nil, `%1\A00x`, []string{"0"})
returned a 102-byte string and no error.

